1785556589253351	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyrAY		item.comment	bn-1hrk	{"body":"PR-3/IMPL-02 landed in workspace @ 36d41f0 (1/2 — import and the component policy). Five new modules, one per operation; zero edits to snapshot.rs/components.rs/publication.rs (lib.rs touched only for the five mod lines + the stale PR-1 scaffold-prose refresh).\n\nIMPORT (src/import.rs): DiskImporter is the crate's ONLY fs-touching type; everything downstream consumes values. The walk is iterative (explicit stack, no recursion => an adversarial tree costs time, never the call stack), depth-first with each directory's entries sorted by raw name bytes BEFORE any is opened, so the importer's own rejections (I/O, unrepresentable name) are reported for the least entry in path order while admission's come from admit()'s raw-name rule — two orders, both functions of the tree, documented as two rather than pretended to be one. Classification is 1:1 into Candidate; symlinks are checked FIRST via symlink_metadata, so a link to a directory is a link and the walk cannot leave the root. NO path policy is re-decided here: normalization, case, non-UTF-8, reserved segments, depth and symlinks are all consumed from bn-1qhe's admit(). Typed errors: ImportError::{RootIsNotADirectory, Io{path,operation,kind:io::ErrorKind}, UnrepresentableName, AmbiguousComponent, Admission, Snapshot, Descriptor}. Io carries ErrorKind and NOT the OS message — platform/locale strings inside a PartialEq error are a determinism hazard in test and in evidence.\n\nRECOGNIZED-COMPONENT POLICY (the named, closed constant): RECOGNIZED_COMPONENT_FILES: [(ComponentKind, &[&str]); 3] = Dependencies->[Cargo.lock], Toolchain->[rust-toolchain.toml, rust-toolchain], Configuration->[continuum.toml]; COMPONENT_RECOGNITION_VERSION: u32 = 1, which moves whenever the table does (the table decides which workspace gets which descriptor identity). Root-relative only (depth==1; a nested Cargo.lock is just a file in the tree). Ambiguity is a TYPED REFUSAL: both rustup spellings present => ImportError::AmbiguousComponent, never a precedence rule — a precedence rule would make the toolchain identity a function of a preference list rather than of the workspace. Recognition is ADDITIVE, never subtractive: the file stays an ordinary file in the source tree (a snapshot describes the directory) and the component is a second, separately-framed identity over the same bytes, so the two handles differ and cannot be conflated. Two stated v1 limits, so they are not mistaken for oversights: (a) Cargo.toml is NOT the configuration file — plan §4.2's Rust configuration surface is the [package.metadata.continuum] SECTION of the manifest, extracting a section needs a TOML parser this crate's boundary refuses, and taking the whole manifest would move the configuration identity whenever an unrelated dependency bound changed (a false invalidation); the plan names the standalone spelling continuum.toml for model-only projects and that is what is recognized, with the manifest-section surface left to cargo continuum, which has a parser. (b) The table is Rust-only — ComponentKind::Dependencies is ONE buffer and a polyglot workspace has several lockfiles at once, so widening it first requires deciding how several ecosystems compose into one component identity, which is a decision, not a list. Callers who want an unrecognized component still supply bytes through WorkspaceDescriptorBuilder, which is docs/35's 'client submits dependency/config references'.\n\nEMPTY DIRECTORIES (bn-15gj's open question): answered NO — import SKIPS a directory that contributes no file, and the record grammar does NOT move. Representing them would change every directory record in every existing snapshot to say something nothing downstream of a snapshot reads (nothing reaches a directory except to reach a file), and a store-format change is not paid for by a feature nobody consumes. The cost is stated rather than hidden: a workspace whose meaning depends on an empty directory existing (a mount point, an output dir a build expects) does not round-trip through a snapshot, and the honest fix is a marker file, which IS content. Skipping is not silent: ImportedWorkspace::unrepresented_directories() names every directory the walk entered that left no trace, by DISK path — because a directory with no file under it has no workspace path, which is exactly what 'not representable' means. Also decided and documented: the importer applies NO ignore policy at all (no .gitignore reading, no .git special case, no build-output heuristic). An exclusion policy makes snapshot identity a function of files that are not in the snapshot, and .gitignore semantics would have to be implemented identically by every future importer or one workspace has two identities.\n\nbn-1qhe's open seam — is the policy in force recorded beside the components? Decided: the import result REPORTS admission_policy() and component_recognition_version() so a caller can record them beside what it publishes, but neither enters any record. bn-2xri's descriptor grammar is closed ('closed kind, versioned growth') and adding a field would re-derive every existing identity; and today exactly one symlink arm is implemented and one recognition table exists, so the policy cannot vary between two importers of one workspace. Binding them into a published workspace-snapshot document is schema-assembly work for whichever PR performs it."}	blake3:55nJG0MKTEvHq_MowdPKAhVFJGQM3K_5ME8kqK-Qjl8
1785556620064231	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyrQY		item.comment	bn-1hrk	{"body":"PR-3/IMPL-02 @ 36d41f0 (2/2 — overlay, fork, seal, diff; tests and gates).\n\nOVERLAY (src/overlay.rs): Overlay{BTreeMap writes, BTreeSet removals} + derive(&Snapshot, &I) -> OverlaySnapshot. No &mut and no interior mutability anywhere in the module, so the base's immutability is a property of the signatures rather than a promise in prose. An overlay is a SET, not a script: duplicate write, duplicate removal, and write-and-remove of one path are typed refusals, so the value is a function of the set of calls and never of their order (the same discipline WorkspaceContent::insert applies). Shadowing a base file is the whole point and is not a duplicate; removing a path the base does not hold is OverlayError::RemovedPathAbsent — a disagreement about what the base contains is reported, not absorbed. OverlaySnapshot carries the base identity (the schema's 'parent') and is_overlaid(path), which is the schema's files[].overlay — closing snapshot.rs's 'overlay provenance is IMPL-02's' note. Provenance is NOT identity: an overlaid file and the same bytes imported from disk share one identity (tested), which is what keeps an overlay snapshot a first-class snapshot rather than a second kind of thing.\n\nFORK (src/lineage.rs): Fork{ForkName, origin, parent: Option, head} — the typed operation plus provenance, not copying. Three provenance questions kept separate: origin (the divergence point, fixed at diverge() and never moved), parent (the head this head superseded — the schema's 'parent', ADR-0018's SUPERSEDES one step at a time), and identity (the head's own name). advance_to()/advance() return a NEW Fork value, so a caller holding the earlier value still holds the earlier line. A ForkName is a LABEL and appears in no record: two forks of one snapshot under two names have the same head identity (tested) — letting a name reach identity would give one workspace content two artifact names.\n\nSEAL (src/seal.rs): SealedWorkspace::seal(descriptor, &ReferenceStore, &CapabilityToken) publishes descriptor.records() in that order (children-before-parents) with no reordering, filtering, or deduplicating, and returns a value carrying the descriptor plus one receipt per record. Per record it checks receipt.handle() == the identity the descriptor derived, else SealError::IdentityDisagreement: two identity seams disagreeing means the bytes sit under a name the descriptor never uses, so it is refused loudly rather than reconciled. Convergence is the STORE's — this module has no cache and no already-published set, because a dedup this module could observe would be an existence oracle (plan §4.5). rebind_components(&descriptor) -> WorkspaceDescriptorBuilder is the fork->seal step (an editor buffer does not change the lockfile, and a sealed workspace has no disk to re-read); it is a plain fn in my module, so components.rs stays untouched. Explicitly out of scope and documented: no intent is attached (docs/35 step 5 needs PR 4's in_*), and no workspace-snapshot JSON document is published (that needs the epochs and the intent reference).\n\nDIFF (src/diff.rs): WorkspaceDiff::between(before, after) builds on Snapshot::diff and adds the content identities on BOTH sides (FileChange{path, kind, before: Option<ArtifactHandle>, after: Option<...>}, Some/None exactly as the kind implies), carrying identity_collisions() forward unchanged — a number dropped one layer up is a number suppressed. of_overlay(base, derived) checks that base IS the derivation's base and refuses BaseMismatch otherwise; that is deliberately NOT the stale-snapshot error (a separate deliverable) but the narrower 'these two values were never related'. Rich semantic diff stays out and says so: a move is a removal plus an addition.\n\nTESTS (+64). 31 new lib unit tests (import 8, overlay 8, lineage 6, diff 5, seal 4; lib total 110 -> 141), 5 new doctests (import's is no_run — type-checked, no fs side effects), and 28 new integration tests across two REAL-TEMPDIR files (no tempfile crate: std::env::temp_dir + a per-test subdir named after the test, removed before AND after, so a panicking run cannot poison the next).\ntests/disk_import.rs (16): import determinism (one tree written in two orders => identical snapshot AND descriptor identity, plus re-import of the same directory); recognition of all three components with contents; recognition-is-additive (file in the tree and a component, distinct identities, equal bytes); a missing component moves the descriptor identity; ambiguous toolchain refusal; symlink refusal naming link and target, and FollowWithCycleDetection still UnsupportedSymlinkPolicy through the importer; a real socket (UnixListener::bind) => AdmissionError::Special; a non-UTF-8 name (OsStrExt, guarded) => PathError::SegmentEncoding{index:0,offset:3,byte:0xff}; empty-dir skip including a nested chain named by disk path, with the identity equal to the tree written without them; the depth bound typed on BOTH sides (MAX_DEPTH imports, MAX_DEPTH+1 => TooDeep, no stack overflow); the segment bound is the platform's (255 imports; 256 cannot be created on a conforming filesystem, and is refused typed where it can); NFC/NFD names stay two files (guarded on a normalizing fs); an empty root is a legal workspace; file-as-root and absent-root are typed; an unreadable directory => Io{ReadDirectory, PermissionDenied}.\ntests/workspace_lifecycle.rs (12): the base is bit-for-bit unchanged after a derive (compared against a clone taken before, encode() included); an overlay snapshot equals the same content imported directly; two clients fork and evolve independently with the untouched subtree keeping the base's identity; the fork provenance chain; seal publishes children-before-parents, everything is readable under the names it carries, fsck clean; two seals of identical trees converge (published_count unchanged, BOTH publications receipted); two forks publish their shared subtree once; the diff names both sides of add/remove/modify; a ConstantIdentity collision is surfaced by the diff AND refused by the store (Aborted, never conflated); import -> fork -> overlay -> rebind -> seal end to end from a real directory; and an old snapshot surviving the working tree (edit + delete + add on disk; it still decodes to itself and still holds the deleted file). The depth test uses the Fnv1aTestIdentity stand-in from merkle_snapshot.rs for the stated reason: HexIdentity is injective but not fixed-width, so a 64-deep tree doubles the identity at every level.\n\nGATES: just check exit 0 (fmt, clippy -D warnings, workspace tests, boundaries, governance code/ADR/claims, dossier, lean) — run before and again after the annotation. START_HERE PR-3 IMPL-02 bullet annotated '(delivered: bn-1hrk)'; traceability regenerated (PR-3-IMPL-02 active -> satisfied; pr-deliverable 120 -> 119 active and 26 -> 27 delivered; registry 748 -> 747 active). Plan §21 PHASE-A-DEL-01 deliberately NOT annotated: the workspace half completes here, the INTENT schemas are PR 4, so the label stays carried on this bone for the lead to move. notes/plan/validation-results.json is now stale by one (active_requirements 748 -> 747); left untouched for the post-wave regen."}	blake3:buSj_E3OKeQ0GtPMKCI-kqM6Pi40cQPxGxKw90gtIss
