1785557787872859	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEytQY		item.comment	bn-3tda	{"body":"PR-3/IMPL-04 landed in workspace @ fafc221. One new module, crates/continuum-workspace/src/staleness.rs (747 lines); zero edits to lineage.rs/overlay.rs/seal.rs/diff.rs/snapshot.rs — lib.rs touched only for the mod line plus a doc-paragraph update recording staleness's place among the crate's five operations.\n\nSTALENESS MODEL: check_current(fork: &Fork, identity: &ArtifactHandle) -> Result<(), LineageError> is the typed ask. Ok when identity equals fork's current head (content equality decides current-ness, not step count — advancing to an equal-content head per lineage.rs's own doc note still passes). Err(LineageError::Stale(StaleSnapshot)) when identity equals fork.origin() or fork.parent() and is not the head. Err(LineageError::Unknown(UnknownSnapshot)) otherwise — a DISTINCT type, not a variant tag on one struct (INV-008: matching one arm cannot yield the other's shape).\n\nPrecisely scoped to what a Fork value itself remembers: per lineage.rs's own \"Provenance is three questions, not one\", a single Fork carries only origin (fixed forever), one step of parent, and the current head — never the whole advance chain. check_current can only PROVE staleness for identity == origin or identity == parent; an identity that was briefly a head two-plus advances back and has since fallen out of the Fork value's own memory is reported Unknown — an honest \"this Fork value cannot place it\" rather than a false \"never existed\" or a silently-accepted \"current\". Documented explicitly in the module doc and exercised by a dedicated test (origin(base)->A->B->C: check against the final Fork proves origin and B stale, C current, and reports A — invisible to that Fork value — as Unknown, not a false Stale).\n\nERROR SHAPE: StaleSnapshot{stale, current, fork: ForkName} and UnknownSnapshot{identity, current, fork: ForkName} — deliberately NOT an embedded Fork clone. First cut embedded the whole Fork (168 bytes, since it carries a full Snapshot) and clippy::result_large_err (part of -D warnings) correctly flagged every guarded function's Result as oversized; the caller already holds the &Fork it passed to check_current, so handing back a second copy bought no recovery capability, only weight. ForkName rides along for a readable message only (lineage.rs: \"a fork's name is a label, never an identity\") — never compared as identity anywhere.\n\nGUARDED ENTRY POINTS (new variants; unguarded forms in overlay.rs/lineage.rs/seal.rs untouched and still take any value on the caller's own coordination, per instruction): derive_current(overlay, base, fork, identifier) guards Overlay::derive against a superseded base; advance_to_current/advance_current(fork, expected_head, ...) guard Fork::advance_to/advance as compare-and-set (refuse when expected_head is no longer fork's actual head — an editor-concurrency scenario, not the repair.promote wire op, which RFC 0032 correction 3 gives StatusConflict instead, so I did not cite that as StaleSnapshot precedent); seal_current(descriptor, fork, store, capability) guards SealedWorkspace::seal against a descriptor whose source has been superseded, refusing before spending a publication. Each has its own composed error enum (GuardedOverlayError/GuardedAdvanceError/GuardedSealError = Lineage(LineageError) | <underlying error>).\n\nWIRE ALIGNMENT: cites IDL's StaleSnapshot doc comment verbatim (\"the named snapshot is not the current one, or is not sealed where sealing is required\") and scopes this crate to the first clause only — the second (durability/sealed-ness) is PR 5's daemon question, this crate's Snapshot values are already immutable regardless of seal.rs's durability. Cites rule errors.common (any operation naming a non-null snapshot MAY return StaleSnapshot — matches check_current's signature exactly: a Fork plus a named identity), RFC 0026's error taxonomy row and Resume decision table (\"the pinned snapshot is no longer the current sealed snapshot\"), RFC 0027 H8 (\"a stale-handle failure is recoverable and says so\") and its scenario 3 (\"an operation naming the old sealed snapshot returns StaleSnapshot with a recovery list that re-bases\"), and the R3 spike report §3 / spikes/agent_protocol_spike.py's proven artifact shape (\"rejection of continuation under a different workspace snapshot\"). No protocol dependency taken (no ErrorCode, no NextOperation) per lib.rs's dependency-boundary contract — confirmed by boundaries gate.\n\ndiff.rs's BaseMismatch distinction (already documented there as \"not the stale-snapshot error\") is restated from this side: BaseMismatch is \"these two values were never related\" (nothing to redo); StaleSnapshot is \"right lineage, stale coordinate\" (carries current() to redo the step against).\n\nTESTS: 9 new unit tests in staleness.rs (lib total 141 -> 150) + 1 doctest (crate total 12 -> 13, all green). Coverage: current-head passes; superseded head -> Stale with exact stale/current identities and the new head named; unrelated identity -> Unknown (distinct type, and Display text checked to not say \"superseded\"); staleness is per-lineage (same snapshot current in an unadvanced fork, stale in a sibling fork advanced from the same base); origin-vs-doubly-superseded-head distinction (the \"honest Unknown\" case above); determinism (Fork reached via the one-call advance() spelling vs the two-call derive()-then-advance_to() spelling — lineage.rs's own documented equivalence — yield identical check_current verdicts); guarded overlay (refuses stale base, derives against current); guarded advance, both spellings (refuses stale expected_head, steps against current); guarded seal (refuses stale descriptor source before publishing, seals a current one, snapshot_identity matches).\n\nGATES: just check exit 0 (fmt, clippy -D warnings incl. the result_large_err fix, workspace tests — 150 lib + all integration suites + all doctests green, boundaries, governance code/ADR/claims, dossier, lean). START_HERE PR-3 IMPL-04 bullet annotated '(delivered: bn-3tda)'; traceability regenerated (pr-deliverable 119->118 active, 27->28 satisfied; registry 747->746 active). validation-results.json/VALIDATION_REPORT.md untouched, not redirected into.\n\nCONCERNS FOR THE LEAD: (1) A Fork value's own bounded memory (origin/parent/head only) means check_current cannot prove staleness for an identity more than one advance behind parent — by design and documented, but worth the lead's eyes since a future caller expecting \"any past identity in this lineage is caught\" would be surprised; the fix if ever needed is store-side (walking sealed publications' parent chain), not a Fork API change. (2) I did not touch crates/continuum-intent/ (sibling agent's disjoint scope) or any of lineage.rs/overlay.rs/seal.rs/diff.rs/snapshot.rs — confirmed by git diff --stat before commit."}	blake3:wgfl3X56Mf_o5JBt8Ut2fcIPRu1BThjbC1IVi0CMYao
1785557846227582	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEytgY		item.comment	bn-3tda	{"body":"Landed @ ad57743. Lead ran all 11 workspace suites in-workspace (150 lib green). Honest-scope decision RATIFIED: a Fork value proves staleness only for origin/parent/head, so a doubly-superseded identity reports Unknown rather than a false Stale — correct at this layer. NOTE FOR PR 5: the daemon's lineage registry must carry FULL supersession history to give wire-accurate StaleSnapshot for deep ancestors; the crate-level Unknown is not the wire answer for a snapshot the daemon knows it superseded. Also ratified: declining the plan.typ citation (RFC 0032 correction 3 supersedes it with StatusConflict for repair.promote) — exactly the RFC-beats-plan discipline. Guarded variants compose; unguarded originals untouched."}	blake3:9yw1LGcJ9bqoV-1KMcUt9VMK9-HPbkUeU07ZCY2_qmQ
