1785597096693013	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3gY		item.move	bn-1tgp	{"state":"done"}	blake3:QnLc6RyVD7Lg3AkhY4uivZAVYH_dpkoa3i1JdCWLi6o
1785597265775544	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy3wY		item.comment	bn-7f23	{"body":"PR-4 observers/faults/fairness (bn-7f23, bn-111k, bn-14w6) — shared-module seam noted, designed around rather than taken.\n\nfairness[].condition is a BARE $defs/formula, so the group must READ a property AST. The crate's only property-AST reader is property.rs's private decode_formula/decode_expression, reachable solely through the public property::Claim::from_json. Opening a second formula decoder in fairness.rs would give one grammar two readers — the way a document comes to have two meanings — so FairnessConstraint::from_json wraps the condition in a minimal in-memory claim ({expression:{ast:<condition>}, id, kind:safety, observer:null}) and takes the AST back out. The wrapper is built from constants and never parsed, so no wrapper-level rejection is reachable; every error a caller sees is the AST's own, named by the AST's own field paths (formula.kind, compare.op, term.kind, action.modality) and surfaced as FairnessDecodeError::Condition(PropertyDecodeError). tests/fairness_adversarial_decode.rs sweeps all four of those vocabularies through this decoder as evidence the reuse is real.\n\nThe clean form is a public ast::Formula::from_json (or a public property::PropertyExpression::from_json). Both are edits to files this bone may not touch while the two sibling field-group bones are in flight, so this is left as a follow-up for the lead, not taken here.\n\nSecond, smaller seam: each of observers.rs / faults.rs / fairness.rs defines its own identity newtype (ObserverIdentity, FaultModelIdentity, FairnessIdentity) because property::PropertyIdentity::of_bytes is module-private and a shared identity module would be a shared line in lib.rs. They are byte-for-byte the same discipline (ADR-0013: the identity IS the canonical preimage bytes; digest<H>() only indexes) and collapse into one type whenever a later bone can touch lib.rs."}	blake3:1WfnDcnEIScKEDlU-9g1sFIceW5gN1U6spbxEs-uvjU
1785597281671807	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4AY		item.comment	bn-7f23	{"body":"Ordering rule adopted for uniqueItems/keyed arrays, so the other field-group bones can match it.\n\nproperty::ClaimSet already flags that ID5 fixes the order of OBJECT KEYS and says nothing about arrays, and that every group with a declared unit key needs the same answer. The answer taken here, in all three groups: an array whose members are a set (uniqueItems) or are keyed by a declared unit key is encoded ASCENDING BY THE RFC 0031 UNIT LOCATOR, in Unicode code-point order — the same rule ID5 states for keys. Consequences:\n\n- observers[] sorts by id; each projection's elements sort by code point.\n- fault_model.enabled and .profiles sort by the wire token. FaultClass therefore implements Ord as wire()-order, NOT schema enum-declaration order; FaultClass::ALL keeps declaration order for enumeration. Encoded: [crash,delay,duplication,loss,partition,recovery].\n- fairness[] sorts by FairnessKey = (kind, action), kind by wire token (strong < weak).\n\nReordering an authored array is then a reformat and does not move the identity, which is what ID7 requires of a reformat. If a sibling group (scope.fragments, trust_boundaries, optimization.*, accepted_evidence_classes, nondeterminism, abstraction_maps, assumptions) chose enum-declaration order instead, the two will disagree and the lead should harmonize before the contract-assembly bone composes them.\n\nTwo RFC flags raised in module docs, both worth a correction pass:\n1. RFC 0037's observers row reads 'event families plus the four projection kinds of plan §5.2', which counts five. Plan §5.2, the schema, and RFC 0031's comparison rule all say four, of which 'events' is one.\n2. semantic-diff.schema.json spells the faults unit locator as 'the member of enabled or profiles' — bare, so a domain-pack profile named 'crash' and the fault class 'crash' render the same locator despite being different units with different policy consequences (W8 applies to one, not the other). Implemented as the schema spells it (INV-003), with FaultUnit::field() carrying the qualification beside it."}	blake3:EwAlTOrmqclT9NlVqmdYopYcbmAs2c3pgE7n_B9CpxQ
1785597489513870	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4QY		item.comment	bn-7f23	{"body":"Landed @ d909575. Lead ran all 12 crate suites in-workspace (green). Design ratified: four uniform projection sets compared componentwise (events not privileged — matches RFC 0031 against RFC 0037's own miscounted row), no fault-envelope scalar (per-member visibility is what makes no-removal enforceable), fairness None-condition as antitone extremum, W4 enforced on both document and constructor paths with the author's own operator named. The <4KB length guards on amplification inputs are exactly the right immune response to the OOM incident. Concern routing: fairness claim-wrapper + three identity newtypes → bn-3lfmi (scope extended); array-ordering harmonization → recorded for the contract-assembly/exit bone; RFC 0037 five-vs-four observers row + faults locator ambiguity → new corrections bone."}	blake3:DpHCdLDry4Z2aQc6q2bYA2iO5wBhpTXw8XmqjT_7l-s
1785597489550690	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4gY		item.move	bn-7f23	{"state":"done"}	blake3:7fS_YoaQL7gkpI1I8jFmKKFGK4b3NIFxzFQ7x9aKJ5c
1785597489579722	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy4wY		item.move	bn-111k	{"state":"done"}	blake3:jnNld8wUwFCGa1qOIAf84N3cxdUerZWD8l7BS5LwuZc
1785597489608830	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5AY		item.move	bn-14w6	{"state":"done"}	blake3:Q12G0NUkZkv7sjZaKUE6ONouP6LVOMicr4IKcacI4Sw
1785597489638012	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5QY		item.comment	bn-3lfmi	{"body":"Scope extended by bn-7f23's landing: (1) public ast::Formula::from_json (or PropertyExpression::from_json) so fairness.rs drops its minimal-claim wrapper around the private decoder; (2) collapse ObserverIdentity/FaultModelIdentity/FairnessIdentity (+ assumptions' copy) into one shared pub(crate) identity module — all four are the same ADR-0013 discipline verbatim. The adversarial suites remain the behavioral spec: zero behavior change."}	blake3:EyMAVH67UruH7ExR6H2QbvzdfsGT1wLaXiXfxyoAXwU
1785597489801337	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5gY		item.create	bn-16pyr	{"description":"# Context\nTwo defects surfaced by bn-7f23's trio (module docs carry the full analysis):\n1. RFC 0037's observers row says 'event families plus the four projection kinds' — counts FIVE where plan §5.2, intent-contract.schema.json, and RFC 0031 all count FOUR (events IS one of the four sets). Correct the RFC row per the RFC-beats-plan-but-schema-beats-RFC discipline (INV-003).\n2. semantic-diff.schema.json's faults unit locator is the bare member name, so a profile named 'crash' and the class 'crash' render identically despite different policy consequences (W8 applies to one only). Decide: qualify the locator in the schema (breaking for the draft epoch — allowed pre-freeze) or record the ambiguity as accepted with the field() disambiguation the implementation carries.\n3. Collect any further RFC 0037 textual defects the policy trio (bn-fp0g wave) surfaces.\n\n# Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] RFC 0037 observers row corrected with direction recorded.\n- [ ] Faults locator decision taken and recorded in schema + RFC 0031 (or explicitly accepted with rationale).\n- [ ] Dossier validator green.","kind":"task","title":"RFC 0037 corrections from PR-4 field-group implementation"}	blake3:JjDxIvQvcFNdG5xil8dH3GI5m7lFXYMopiLF9pP6pqw
1785597504462911	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy5wY		item.update	bn-16pyr	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"docs"}}	blake3:jeUl-V3ig2llatthP59i5EEF9iyZ_tctLwDVney6-iw
1785597504491812	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6AY		item.update	bn-16pyr	{"field":"labels","value":{"action":"add","label":"req:pr-4"}}	blake3:Jxj6XlmDcjN3xCLvgKrIe7VM23NwFXPZgPM6RlOfysc
1785597792097947	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6QY		item.comment	bn-fp0g	{"body":"PR-4 IMPL-07/08/09 landed in .maw/workspaces/bn-fp0g (commit 777079b). Three modules: assurance_policy.rs (AssurancePolicy over continuum-value's AssuranceLevel/AssuranceRequirement/AssuranceChange/EvidenceClass — no parallel enum), optimization.rs (Objective/NonVacuityObligation/OptimizationUnit, two policy keys structurally separated per RFC 0037 correction 5), change_policy.rs (PolicyField x15, PolicyVerb x8, Relation x16, PolicyTable/PolicyReviewers, W6/W7, P1-P6 verdict, policy joins with typed conflict). 102 new tests (13 in-module, 89 integration across 6 new tests/ files); crate suite 168 total. just check exit 0; validator pass; traceability regenerated (3 pr-deliverables flipped to satisfied). No AssuranceLevel discrepancy: the schema enum and crates/continuum-value/src/assurance.rs agree member-for-member and order-for-order, asserted mechanically by parsing the schema with the crate's own reader. Findings raised for the lead: (1) schemas/examples/intent-contract.example.json violates W7 — nine fields carry the review verb and the instance has no policy_reviewers key; (2) RFC 0031's per-field admissible-relation table omits 'unknown' from five membership-only rows while its fail-closed rule requires it everywhere — implemented as always-admissible; (3) RFC 0037 'Policy joins' illustration is narrower than its own definition (locked join v = locked; review join proposal-only = review); (4) neither RFC states what an assurance checker-flag declaredness change classifies — change_to returns a typed refusal for the classifier to record as unknown; (5) policy_reviewers principal arrays carry minItems but no uniqueItems while being in the ID2 preimage — normalized (dedup+sort) rather than rejected; (6) no per-claim assurance key exists in the schema, so the demand is contract-wide."}	blake3:J6D_AbQ4bpOwvlgvvcEkmBByOWccCsW2iEJ4eXo7u2w
1785597809491619	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6gY		item.comment	bn-fp0g	{"body":"Correction to the test count in the previous comment: the crate suite is 201 passing tests after this change (81 lib incl. 13 new in-module; 23 adversarial_decode; 8 pr4_property_evidence; 89 across the six new tests/ files). 102 of those are new."}	blake3:TiQWHV40JyHs3pZ61xgmGq5H7uLovmwRZv6Itt8_6o4
1785597913690939	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy6wY		item.comment	bn-fp0g	{"body":"Landed @ d2d64ea. Lead ran all 18 crate suites in-workspace (201 tests green). Design ratified: continuum-value's assurance ladder imported not mirrored (member/token/order agreement asserted mechanically); Option<bool> checker flags with no invented defaults; hard/soft/non-vacuity as distinct types with no flattening accessor (correction 5's enforceability argument); verb→field table verified against the schema's own $defs by parsing the schema with this crate's reader — transcription distrust done right; typed PolicyJoinConflict instead of rounding. All six findings routed to bn-16pyr."}	blake3:KRYkqeRA4WcUncHx8Yhuod-iGjgU7PvRFECcfDreTJI
1785597913721688	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7AY		item.move	bn-fp0g	{"state":"done"}	blake3:1-xFBPhwqZWnVvjmbE-21PNAF0OSeDJ7js28Vw-UBtE
1785597913750339	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7QY		item.move	bn-1fc0	{"state":"done"}	blake3:svfJGcFovA-CF5GAmEbwV1QuA38FwKuaCCnwK1IgLUw
1785597913778864	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7gY		item.move	bn-185wn	{"state":"done"}	blake3:gXovufKPZcAONVndbKbcctzWEjeBOj0rOApNz5EJLII
1785597913808387	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy7wY		item.comment	bn-16pyr	{"body":"Scope extended by bn-fp0g's landing — six adjudication items (full analysis in the three module docs and the bone report): (1) intent-contract.example.json violates W7 (nine fields carry review, no policy_reviewers key) — schema-valid but checker-rule-violating; a pinning test currently asserts the key is ABSENT and must be updated when fixed. (2) RFC 0031 self-conflict: per-field admissible-relation table omits unknown from five membership-only rows while the fail-closed rule requires it — implemented as non-affirmative-everywhere; correct the table. (3) RFC 0037 policy-joins illustration narrower than its own definition (locked ⊔ v, review ⊔ proposal-only) — correct the illustration. (4) Neither RFC classifies an assurance checker-flag declaredness change — implementation returns CheckerDeclarednessChanged → unknown; RFC should state it. (5) policy_reviewers arrays lack uniqueItems in the ID2 preimage — schema fix (draft epoch); implementation normalizes meanwhile. (6) Note: no per-claim assurance key exists by design — record as intended reading."}	blake3:M4cDlhkU8pZdVplEn_Qh3Lu3jmk75Er2nyge2RTQUWA
1785597945129848	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8AY		item.move	bn-3lfmi	{"state":"doing"}	blake3:ypYG08t2S9azwUYryBNr_dLypzpHixq4xl70kblUg8s
1785597945142346	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8QY		item.assign	bn-3lfmi	{"action":"assign","agent":"continuum-dev"}	blake3:CVZR7vczagW9cYFPBbAM2u6sFDgV-4Z8gUGcgXqK_aQ
1785599019016902	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8gY		item.comment	bn-3lfmi	{"body":"Landed @ fe7aeb9. Lead verified: 18 suites green in-workspace, ZERO test-file edits (the 384-test spec ran verbatim), net −313 lines. Ratified: nine distinct public identity newtypes over one shared CanonicalIdentity (collapsing to one public type would let an observer identity typecheck where a claim's is expected — the macro is the right altitude); the Formula::from_json (as-authored) vs PropertyExpression::from_json (normalize+verify) split; fairness's documented double-normalization as the price of pinned error variants. The FieldDecodeError trait sketch is recorded for the assembly bone, with its two stated caveats."}	blake3:JxWPo_WMo-Em9XRtwvCjGRvZ0dfEg9cZeVy5iLMhUaw
1785599019045360	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEy8wY		item.move	bn-3lfmi	{"state":"done"}	blake3:nS9OiWMudfmBNOWPOV0DoarFZzTuVovDfyaDHh18754
1785599468087457	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAQ		item.comment	bn-ces7	{"body":"Orientation done. Read RFC 0037 (ID1/ID2/ID5, W1-W10, the 20-key field table), intent-contract.schema.json's top level, all nine landed field modules + codec.rs + identity.rs, the PR-2/PR-3 exit-evidence pattern, and the two corpus fixtures (notes/plan/corpus/tla-examples/ports/TV-009/ for Die Hard; notes/plan/examples/replicated_register.ctm + .scenario.toml + .md for the replicated register).\n\nSCOPE FINDING the plan's nine bullets do not name: the schema's top level has TWENTY keys and the nine landed modules cover only ten of them (claims, assumptions, observers, bounds, fault_model, fairness, assurance, optimization, policy, policy_reviewers). SIX contract groups have no landed module -- scope, trust_boundaries, completion_policy, nondeterminism, abstraction_maps, security_policy -- plus the four header/metadata keys schema_id, schema_epoch, intent_id, name. All six are simple (string sets, closed enums, scalars, two keyed arrays) and none carries a property AST, so contract.rs models them itself rather than opening six new modules; that is the minimum that lets an IntentContract exist at all.\n\nARRAY-ORDERING SURVEY (bn-7f23's recorded seam) -- no group disagrees with any other. Every keyed/set array in all nine groups already emits ascending by RFC 0031 unit locator in code-point order: ClaimSet/AssumptionSet BTreeMap<UnitKey,_> (String Ord), ObserverSet BTreeMap<ObserverId,_>, FairnessSet BTreeMap<FairnessKey,_> with FairnessKind::cmp hand-written as wire()-order so (kind,action) sorts exactly as the 'kind:action' locator does, FaultModel BTreeSet<FaultClass> with FaultClass::cmp hand-written as wire()-order plus BTreeSet<ProfileName>, Optimization BTreeSet<Objective>/<NonVacuityObligation>, AssurancePolicy BTreeMap<&'static str wire token, EvidenceClass> (deliberately keyed by token, NOT by EvidenceClass's continuum-value declaration order), PolicyTable BTreeMap<PolicyField,_> rendered through wire(), PolicyReviewers BTreeMap keyed by wire() with BTreeSet<String> principals. Two hand-written Ord impls and one wire-token-keyed BTreeMap are what make it coherent; contract.rs will document the rule crate-wide and pin it with a test.\n\nONE ORDERING TRAP for the groups I own: ast::Fragment derives Ord, so BTreeSet<Fragment> is DECLARATION order (Finite,Symbolic,Temporal,Probabilistic,Theorem,Runtime), not code-point order (Finite,Probabilistic,Runtime,Symbolic,Temporal,Theorem). scope.fragments must therefore be encoded through a wire-token-keyed map exactly as assurance_policy.rs does for EvidenceClass -- NOT by iterating the BTreeSet. Fragment's Ord is not changed (it is a landed public behavior and check_fragments' parameter type).\n\nDesign going in: decode = structural + per-group, strict; check = a separate verdict surface carrying the cross-field W-rules (W2 observers-resolve, W3 fragments, W7 review-implies-reviewers, W8 profiles+maps resolve, W9 declared identity, W1 for the two arrays I own). W10 is structural (schema_id/schema_epoch are 'const' in the schema) so it is enforced at decode and recorded as such. The example's W7 violation therefore decodes cleanly and surfaces only as a checker finding, per bn-fp0g/bn-16pyr."}	blake3:KQ-iHs7JL6HmS5yXNvOcg_0sQKPjr_0iiETld-g2s2k
1785600983831411	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAg		item.comment	bn-ces7	{"body":"IMPLEMENTATION LANDED in-workspace (not yet committed). src/contract.rs (~1780 lines incl. 15 in-module tests) + tests/pr4_exit_evidence.rs (22 tests) + two golden fixtures. Crate suite 423 passing (384 pre-existing verbatim + 15 in-module + 22 evidence + 2 doctests). clippy -D warnings clean, fmt clean.\n\nCONTRACT DESIGN.\n- PREIMAGE (ID1/ID2), stated exactly in contract.rs's module doc and CHECKED not asserted: IntentIdentity IS the canonical-JSON bytes of an object carrying exactly EIGHTEEN keys — the schema's twenty top-level keys minus 'name' and minus 'intent_id'. policy_reviewers is PRESENT even when the document omits it (encoded as {}), because ID2 puts it in the preimage and ID5 forbids absent-vs-empty from being two identities for one meaning. Inside claims[] and assumptions[] the expression objects drop 'source' and 'normal_form' — not re-derived here, taken from ClaimSet/AssumptionSet::identity_preimage_json (two NEW public methods, pure additions, exposing the Layer::Identity spelling those modules already used for their own identities). $comment never appears at any depth because no encoder emits one and every reader rejects unknown keys. tests/pr4_exit_evidence.rs walks the preimage recursively and fails if source/normal_form/$comment is reachable, checks 'name'/'intent_id' absent at top level only (they are also AST keys deeper down), and asserts preimage.to_canonical_bytes() == identity().canonical_bytes().\n- ORDERING RULE, stated once for the crate in contract.rs: an array whose members are a set (uniqueItems) or are keyed by a declared RFC 0031 unit key is encoded ASCENDING BY THAT UNIT LOCATOR IN CODE-POINT ORDER — the rule ID5 already states for object keys. Module doc carries the full fifteen-group table.\n- W-RULES AT CONTRACT LEVEL: W2 (claims->observers), W3 (scope.fragments -> both AST-carrying groups), W7 (policy->policy_reviewers), W8 (fault profiles -> domain packs; abstraction_maps -> the §16 graph), W9 (intent_id -> the contract's own canonical encoding). Surface: check(&CheckEnvironment) -> ContractVerdict, a VERDICT not an error, with typed ContractFinding variants each carrying its WellFormednessRule and field paths. W1/W4/W6 are per-group and decided at construction; W10 (schema_id/schema_epoch) is STRUCTURAL — both are 'const' in the schema — so it is a decode rejection (HeaderMismatch), recorded as such. W5 is NOT decided here: it quantifies over the model's declared free variables, which is a continuum-model fact this crate is below.\n- FAIL-CLOSED: CheckEnvironment has no 'skip this rule' spelling. No profiles supplied => a declared profile is unresolved (W8's own instruction). No minted handle supplied => DeclaredIdentityUnverified, never a pass. mint_intent_id::<H>() is hasher-parameterized so the crate still makes no hash-vendor decision; the certified identity remains the canonical bytes and the digest only indexes (ADR-0013).\n- The four rules a landed group already owns are DECIDED BY THAT GROUP (check_observers/check_fragments/check_reviewers/check_profiles) rather than reimplemented, so the verdict names one violation per (rule, group) pair — a fixed one, documented. The two halves this bone owns outright (abstraction_maps under W8, all of W9) report every violation.\n- FieldDecodeError trait sketch (bn-3lfmi) NOT ADOPTED, with the reasoning in the module doc: the nine adversarial suites are the behavioral spec and they match on VARIANTS; boxing erases exactly that, in a crate inside the smallest trust base with no dynamic dispatch anywhere else. Ten typed variants + ten From impls instead.\n\nHARMONIZATION SURVEY RESULT: NO GROUP DISAGREES WITH ANY OTHER. decode(encode(c)) == c and encode is deterministic across all fifteen groups together, proven on both corpus fixtures. Three deliberate choices are what make it coherent and they are named in the doc table: FairnessKind::cmp and FaultClass::cmp are HAND-WRITTEN as wire() order (not derived declaration order), and AssurancePolicy holds its evidence classes in a BTreeMap keyed by wire TOKEN because EvidenceClass is continuum-value's type ordered by RFC 0010 listing order. NO MODULE'S OUTPUT WAS RE-ORDERED. One trap found and avoided in the groups this bone owns: ast::Fragment DERIVES Ord, so BTreeSet<Fragment> iterates declaration order (Finite,Symbolic,Temporal,Probabilistic,Theorem,Runtime), not code-point order (Finite,Probabilistic,Runtime,Symbolic,Temporal,Theorem) — Scope therefore holds fragments in a BTreeMap keyed by wire token, exactly as assurance_policy.rs does, and Fragment's Ord is left alone (landed public behavior, and the parameter type of two check_fragments methods). Pinned by an in-module test using a triple that can tell the two orders apart."}	blake3:RcEWpiNQyxYy1--_-PCAuYz5h9B89Od7kDfJRoPmR_A
1785601006745494	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyAw		item.comment	bn-ces7	{"body":"SCOPE FINDING (needs the lead's eye — it is bigger than the bone title suggests). The plan's nine PR-4 bullets cover TEN of the schema's twenty top-level keys. SIX protected field groups had no owning bone: scope, trust_boundaries, completion_policy, nondeterminism, abstraction_maps, security_policy — plus the four header/metadata keys schema_id, schema_epoch, intent_id, name. RFC 0037's own field table lists all twenty and calls fifteen of them protected; the plan's bullet list is the incomplete one. All six are modelled inside src/contract.rs rather than as six new modules: none carries a property AST, each is a string set / closed enum / scalar / keyed array of two strings, and an IntentContract cannot exist without them. New public types: Scope, TrustBoundaries, CompletionPolicy, ChoiceClass, ChoiceSite, NondeterminismSet, MapRole, MapId, AbstractionMaps, DataClassification, SecurityPolicy, IntentId, ContractParts, IntentContract, IntentIdentity, CheckEnvironment, WellFormednessRule, ContractFinding, ContractVerdict, ContractError, ContractDecodeError. They deliberately do NOT get per-group identity newtypes: the nine landed groups have them because each was its own artifact bone; these six are parts of one document and the contract identity covers ID7 for them.\n\nREPLICATED-REGISTER FIXTURE PROVENANCE. The plan names a replicated register but no intent fields exist in fixture form, so every field is TRANSCRIBED with its source cited at file:line in the test's module doc (a two-column table). Sources: notes/plan/examples/replicated_register.ctm (claims Agreement ctm:48-53 and StableWitness ctm:55-61; fairness weak Recover ctm:63; Crash/Recover ctm:36,42; const Quorum ctm:7; Choose(...,q) ctm:28), replicated_register.scenario.toml (bounds values=2/nodes=3 from [domains] :7-8, faults=2 from max_crashes :24; fault classes loss/duplication :14-15, delay from allow_reordering :16, partition from max_partitions :26; profiles :12,19; observer client from crash_after=\"client.reply:Committed\" :30; RuntimeToAbstract named at :35; completion_policy from asupersync::Quiescence :36), and replicated_register.md (the refinement sentence :129; bounds restated :113-118; assurance from :112/:125/:129/:142; the soft objective verbatim from :106; redaction class crashpack from :133). Three transcriptions are marked AS transcriptions in the table because they are readings rather than quotations: allow_reordering -> the 'delay' fault class (reordering is not in the plan §5.2 vocabulary and delay is the class it is a consequence of); the RuntimeToAbstract claim's observer binding to 'client'; and the network/storage assumptions, which state the RESIDUAL guarantee left after the scenario permits loss/duplication/reordering and volatile-suffix-loss/torn-last-record. One field is marked AUTHORED: the policy table and its policy_reviewers, included deliberately because the dossier's own example is the W7-UNNAMED case and this fixture is the named one. Die Hard's provenance table is the same discipline against corpus/tla-examples/ports/TV-009/{DieHard.ctm,default.model.toml,port.json,README.md}; its three claims are byte-identical to the ones pr4_property_evidence.rs already pins, so the two evidence files agree by construction.\n\nBoth golden fixtures were validated against schemas/intent-contract.schema.json with jsonschema: VALID. That check was run by hand (validate_dossier.py is out of this bone's fence); wiring the two fixtures into the dossier validator's example list would make it mechanical and is worth a follow-up bone. Inside the crate the coupling IS mechanical from the other side: tests/pr4_exit_evidence.rs parses intent-contract.schema.json with this crate's own reader and asserts the schema's twenty declared 'properties' keys equal the twenty a full contract encodes, that additionalProperties is false, and that 'required' has exactly eighteen members excluding name and policy_reviewers."}	blake3:RzP2xw5H1zPO4gQAJ-Gh8Y2NNIBhR6otpGWcivNBOts
1785601113703861	continuum-dev	itc:v3:AWKSTzM5JJnJPkQAAAAAQar23VW6voAAAAEyBA		item.comment	bn-ces7	{"body":"COMMITTED @ 0521c6c in .maw/workspaces/bn-ces7 (not merged, not pushed, no bn state change). just check EXIT 0 end to end (fmt, clippy -D warnings, tests, boundaries, governance, dossier validator, lean+axiom manifest), run ONCE at the end. 904 workspace tests; continuum-intent 423 (384 pre-existing passing VERBATIM — zero edits to any existing test file — plus 15 new in-module, 22 new in tests/pr4_exit_evidence.rs, 2 doctests). Traceability regenerated: PR-4 and PR-4-EXIT flip active -> satisfied; START_HERE PR 4 Exit line annotated '(delivered: bn-ces7)'. Validator output never redirected into validation-results.json.\n\nEXIT SENTENCE, clause by clause:\n- 'Die Hard ... serialize[s] canonically' — the full 20-key contract round-trips to a checked-in golden (tests/fixtures/die-hard-contract.json, 3086 bytes), and a document with every object's keys reversed, every set-valued array reversed, and insignificant whitespace inserted re-canonicalizes to THE SAME BYTES AND THE SAME IDENTITY. The reformat deliberately does NOT reverse arrays beneath claims[].expression / assumptions[].expression / fairness[].condition: N4 orders a junction's operands but CPNF-1 'never reorders args', so reversing those would be a different formula, not a different spelling of one.\n- 'replicated-register intent serializes canonically' — same discipline, second golden (6524 bytes), on a contract that exercises what Die Hard does not: quorum quantifiers with a bound-variable domain, three classified assumptions (one with fidelity_profile deliberately UNDECLARED), an observer a claim actually binds, ALL SIX fault classes (so the wire-order rule is proven whole: crash,delay,duplication,loss,partition,recovery), a fairness constraint, three nondeterminism sites, two declared fragments, and four review verbs with their reviewers named.\n- 'ordinary operations cannot mutate them' — three ways. (1) every_public_operation_leaves_the_contract_bit_identical enumerates EVERY public operation the crate offers on a contract (18 group accessors, identity, both preimage spellings, artifact_json, to_artifact_bytes, mint_intent_id, check, Clone, Debug, PartialEq, decode round-trip) plus every derivation reachable through the lent groups (bounds.relation_to, policy.join, assurance.change_to/evidence_class_changes, optimization.require_non_vacuity, fault_model.units, the four W-rule checkers) and asserts bytes AND identity bit-identical after all of it. (2) no_type_in_this_crate_has_a_mut_self_method scans all 16 crate sources via include_str!, skipping comment lines, and asserts ZERO '&mut self'/'self: &mut' receivers everywhere except canonical_json.rs's private Parser cursor — and asserts that exception is still non-empty, so the carve-out cannot silently become vacuous. It also asserts lib.rs's module count matches the scanned file list, so a module added and not scanned fails. (3) a compile_fail doctest on contract.rs paired with an identical passing one (publication.rs's idiom), so the compile_fail cannot rot into a test of its own preamble.\n- ID7 as a LIST: id7s_checkable_list_holds_for_both_corpus_intents runs both fixtures through all three 'MUST NOT change it' cases (rename, re-handle, rewrite source) and all SIXTEEN 'MUST change it' cases one field group at a time, asserting the count is 16 so a dropped case fails.\n\nADVERSARIAL, all linear-size with len()<8192 guards asserted before each sweep: full 3086-byte truncation sweep (every prefix a typed error, every error rendered, never a panic); an unknown top-level key (typed UnknownField naming the key); a duplicated 'claims' group (JsonError::DuplicateKey — last-writer-wins would have silently discarded every claim); each cross-group W-rule producing its own typed finding (W2 dangling observer, W3 undeclared fragment reported separately for claims AND assumptions, W7 unenforceable review, W8 both halves, W9 unverified); and the ID2 exclusion walk.\n\nSEAMS AND CONCERNS FOR THE LEAD:\n1. SCOPE — the six unowned field groups (previous comment). The plan's nine bullets are the incomplete list; RFC 0037's table is the complete one. Worth reconciling in the plan.\n2. RFC 0037 FLAG (new, for bn-16pyr): the 'Absence, null, and defaults' section enumerates bounds.values/bounds.depth/fairness[].condition as the keys whose absence reads as null, then says 'There are no other defaults' — but it separately gives scope.abstraction_level: null the meaning 'no abstraction level is declared', which is also what an absent key says. Implemented as absent == null, encoded explicitly as null, on ID5's own argument that absent and a declared value must not be two identities for one meaning. The RFC should say so. (The distinction that DOES bite — null vs the empty string — is implemented and pinned by a test.)\n3. policy_reviewers absent vs {} — same reasoning, resolved the same way (always encoded, empty included). Recorded because it moves nothing today but would move every identity if reversed.\n4. TWO PUBLIC UnitKey TYPES with the same name (property::UnitKey and assumptions::UnitKey). Deliberate and correct — an assumption's key cannot typecheck where a claim's is expected — but any caller importing both needs an alias, which the evidence file documents. Same shape as the nine identity newtypes bn-3lfmi ratified.\n5. ContractParts has public fields. It is an authoring bundle consumed BY VALUE; an IntentContract shares no storage with it and there is no path from a contract back to its parts. Documented at the type. Flagging it because 'public fields' reads like a mutation affordance at a glance and it is the one place in the crate that has them.\n6. The verdict reports one finding per (rule, group) for the four rules a landed group owns, because this bone calls those checkers rather than reimplementing them. The dossier example's NINE W7 violations therefore surface as one finding naming one field. If the lead wants all-violations reporting, the right move is to add an all-violations method to each group, not a second W7 here.\n7. The two goldens were validated against intent-contract.schema.json BY HAND (jsonschema, both VALID). Wiring them into validate_dossier.py's example list is out of this bone's fence and is worth a follow-up."}	blake3:M6nWyaFY9XW7Xll2htZH8UQT0D5FygEfh-BLsrThfNs
